Ladybird’s Notification.requestPermission() refuses to prompt from a
document whose origin isn’t its top-level origin, from a document that
isn’t a secure context, and without transient activation, which it
consumes when it does prompt; a refused request resolves with the
current state (“default” when nothing is stored), and so does a
dismissed prompt. WebKit’s Notification::requestPermission() denies the
same three cases with console errors, consuming the activation through
DOMWindow::consumeTransientActivation(), and resolves “denied” for
them. Blink’s Notification::requestPermission() only reports a
discouraged-API violation for a missing gesture and counts the
cross-origin-iframe and insecure cases, leaving the refusals to the
browser’s NotificationPermissionContext. Gecko’s
NotificationPermissionRequest::Run() auto-denies without a gesture
under dom.webnotifications.requireuserinteraction, resolving with the
raw stored state (“default”) and a console error. All three resolve
“default” for a prompt the user dismissed.
